From: grigsby@agames.com (JUMP IN THE FIRE)
Subject: African predators (Was Re: Bring back...)

>   Another interesting and widely misunderstood behavior is the fierce
> confrontational snarl of a large predator, generally taken to mean something
> like "I am about to pounce and eat you up!"  It seems more nearly to be the
> case that such displays have to do with fear or dominance.

Yes.  When stalking prey, an activity that depends entirely on
surprise, a roar is counterproductive.  Any animal (including humans)
that gets roared at is classified as a possible threat.  Your further
behavior may, however, establish your status as food.

>   On the other hand, if a large predator approaches you slowly, with a
> benign or neutral expression on its face, you are probably being stalked, and
> are in serious trouble...

Not always.  African predators (mostly big cats) dislike human flesh
and will generally not attack unless you have just shot at them or
they have a vendetta against humans, usually from surviving being
shot.  Jean-Pierre Hallet (and Carl Akeley, and others who would know)
note that a loud "Boo!" or other suitable ejaculation (no beastie
jokes, please) is sufficient to convince even leopards that you are
not food.  Your mileage for other predators in regions other than the
African savanna may vary -- trained dogs in the USA, for instance.

In any case, running from a predator is not an option.

Rhinos may charge you, but they'll charge anything -- they can't see
clearly beyond 20 feet or so.  They have an equal chance of running
away, and an excellent chance of missing you entirely or stopping
short when it can see you better.  See Hallet's book _Animal Kitabu_
for some entertaining rhino anecdotes.  "Since I was somewhat smaller
than a locomotive, he failed to locate me..."

