From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Extropians as Family

Dan Davis sent me e-mail saying that if I had had an urge to flame him
because of his message (attached below), I most likely had
misunderstood him. I had already deleted this message, but I had it
resent to me in the hopes that more careful analyis would make things
clearer.

His message:

> > For now, the use of the term "boundary problems" is common in the
> > psychology field. Many people have problems understanding what is
> > appropriate and what is inappropriate behavior with respect to other
> > people, what to expect of others, how much to pry, and so on. These
> > folks are said to have "boundary problems."
> > 
> > --Tim May
> 
> Well Tim, I find it odd for a man who speaks of morality as a matter
> of personal taste to talk about "inappropriate" interpersonal
> behaviour. 
> 
> Just goes to show that most discourse is about control of the
> feebleminded who accept imperatives if the right ritual incantations
> are used - words like good, bad, immoral, appropriate are particularly
> powerful magic with such people.

On the first paragraph of Dan's, I don't see the point. "Appropriate"
and "inappropriate" are terms of art, as we like to say on the list,
not absolute moral issues. Thus, we might say that bursting into tears
during a lecture, for example, is "inapproprate behavior," even if it
violates no laws, aggresses against no one in a physical way, and is
contractually allowed. Other such examples abound.

Perhaps things would've been clearer from the start if I'd used the
older term "manners." Perhaps I let the jargon of psychoanalysis--in
which "boundaries" play a large, and I think useful, role--cloud my
language. Put this way, my issue is that some of the "honest and
sincere" emotional admissions on this list, particularly the ones
talked about so far, are simply bad manners. A point Romana Machado
made so eloquently.

The second paragraph, about control of the feebleminded, still
mystifies me. I will take from Dan's e-mail to me that he was not
calling me feebleminded. But was he saying that I am using the term
"appropriate" as a means of controlling the feebleminded?

Dan?

--Tim May

