From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: African predators (Was Re: Bring back...)

grigsby@agames.com comments:

>        African predators (mostly big cats) dislike human flesh
> and will generally not attack unless [...]

  The sources I have read suggest that the issue is perhaps less one of
what "tastes good" and more one of what you (the predator) have been brought
up to regard as "food" -- again, a cultural issue.  I have read of two
instances of systematic man-eating African lions, one described in an old
book called _The_Lions_of_Tsavo_ (I think I have the title right, but I
have forgotten the author) and the other in _Born_Free_.  In both cases
the animals involved were a pair of young males, strong, healthy and in the
prime of life, who had started eating human flesh in unusual circumstances
and evidently found it palatable and widely available.
  In the former case, the British had brought in slaves, I mean, forced
labor -- to build a railroad from A to B, and what with poor sanitation,
harsh conditions and so on, the workers were dying like flies.  Their bodies
were just hauled off into the brush and left to rot -- no burial, funeral
pyre or whatever would have been appropriate in their own traditions.  What
seems to have happened is that the pair of young lions found all this strange
carrion lying around and started eating it.  Presently they noticed there was
more of it, on the hoof (so to speak), over in the railroad camp.  During
the course of a month or three, they carried off several dozen laborers.
The lions were eventually shot.
  The pair mentioned in _Born_Free_ had taken to raiding native livestock
corrals and taking the herdsmen (who were sleeping outside the protected
enclosures) rather than the stock (inside).  For some reason, the natives
believed that these particular lions had some special magic such that they
could not be killed, and hence weren't trying -- be it noted that traditional
African weapons (knives, spears, grenades\\\\\\\\) are more than capable of
coping with lions.
  (Pairs of young lions are quite common, incidentally -- what happens is
that male littermates will bond together and go off in search of a pride
to take over; they then share the role of alpha male.)

  I have often wondered whether the substantial degree of freedom from large
predators that many undeveloped human cultures enjoy stems from long cultural
coevolution on the lines of "we'll bury/burn/elevate our dead and you won't
get into the habit of eating us".  Wolves are another interesting case in
point; there seem to be only one or two well-documented instances in the
history of North America of attacks by healthy wild wolves on adult human
beings.

> ... sufficient to convince even leopards that you are not food ...

  I think it is true that leopards rarely take kills that outmass them
by the proportion that a typical adult human does.  (Wolves often do;
lions are bigger than humans, anyway.)

  In case any followers of this thread were not in the newsgroup when I
last mentioned the following books, let me recommend Mark and Delia Owens's
_Cry_of_the_Kalihari_ and _The_Eye_of_the_Elephant_ as superb books about
modern field ethological encounters with African wildlife -- they worked
with wild African lions, in the open, at ranges down to one meter,
regularly.  I also recommend all of Joy Adamson's books -- let me see,
_Born_Free_, _Living_Free_ and _Forever_Free_ about lions, _The_Spotted_
Sphinx_ (cheetah) and _Queen_of_Shabah_ (last word might be wrong) about
a leopard.  Joy was an amateur interested in studying predators in the
field by raising them as cubs and reestablishing them in the wild.  _Born_
_Free_ was made into a movie; many people know the title song, and have
heard of Elsa the lioness.  Fewer realize that she did it again, with a
cheetah and then with Penny the leopard.  And her husband George's
biography, _My_Pride_and_Joy_ relates similar events.  After a long career
as an African game warden, Adamson retired and spent the last 25 years of
his life working with wild or reintroduced lions in the field...

  These books contain numerous interesting examples of complex behavior
by these animals.  Some of the lion behavior, in particular, seems quite
cognitive.

                         -- Jay Freeman, First Extropian Fast Food (*)


